en:
I'm Akemi Iwashita.
I'm a third-year.
Well then, let me begin a story I know.{p}

You quietly hold out your umbrella to a boy who's waiting out the rain.
It's not rare for that to turn out to be the start of a romance.
I don't like that sort of thing, though.{p}

Even so, Tachibana admired Tsukahara, the way he was.
But being so reserved, she could never bring herself to speak to him first. She only ever watched him from a distance.
And God gave a girl like Tachibana a chance.{p}

Could there be a better chance than this?
If she let it slip by, she'd never have another chance to talk to Tsukahara for the rest of her life.
With that thought, Tachibana walked right up to him without hesitating.{p}

...Heh, I see.
Thank you for answering my question.
Well then, let's go on.{p}

...I see.
So you think someone with a pure heart like that is a little strange.
Well, maybe a lot of people would think so.{p}

Tsukahara was past caring about anything now.
When he pulled the umbrella tip out of his left eye, a gaping black hole was left behind.
The eyeball had been dragged out, nerves and all, and was still skewered on the tip of the umbrella.{p}

Of course you are.
...It would be a lie to say you weren't.
It's normal to be interested in girls, more or less.{p}

She was under the eaves of the same shop as the day before.
She spotted Hata and waved to him.
Drawn by her smile, Hata drifted over to her as if in a daze.{p}

Hata shoved her away with a thump.
It wasn't all that hard.
But she was small.{p}

She staggered out from under the eaves.
The pouring rain soaked her body.
At that instant, a horrible scream rang out.{p}

The next day, it was raining too.
It was creepy to think about the day before, but Hata decided to go past the usual shop.
It was a big shortcut, after all.{p}

Hata ran.
Anyone would have, wouldn't they?
Hata ran and ran until he came out onto the main road.{p}

How indecisive.
You should be able to decide something like that in a snap.
You're a man, aren't you?{p}

My, so you do.
You're more meticulous than I expected.
And yet sometimes you don't even bother opening one...{p}

But I understand how you feel.
Umbrellas are such a nuisance, aren't they?
Still, they're not all bad.{p}

......Oh, what's the matter?
Are you scared?
Heh heh... Shall we go on with the story?{p}

...Heh, I see.
Thank you for answering my question.
Well then, let's go on.{p}

Even when he heard about it, Tsukahara didn't seem to feel a thing.
Of course, he didn't go to her funeral either.
Killing him would be too good for him, wouldn't it?{p}

Exactly.
She was a sweet, devoted girl.
The kind of person who was fooled by someone like Tsukahara and still didn't hold it against him.{p}

...Really?
You think it's normal.
Well, if that's what you think, that's fine.{p}

When he opened it, it turned out to be quite a big umbrella.
Tsukahara started for home under it.
But mixed in with the rhythmic patter of raindrops on the umbrella, there was a strange sound.{p}

"What the...?"
Just as Tsukahara went to check the umbrella,
it snapped shut with tremendous force.{p}
